#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124212 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "1a855d24-cb78-5398-89e1-3bfda6907f36" :question/math-academy-id "e-4787" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L,$ we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x).$\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$\n\n- For each $p(x) \\in L,$ we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x) \\in L.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124318 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/id #uuid "57bf5fb6-02e9-56a1-9641-342123f45406" :question/math-academy-id "e-8229" :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0.$ Which of the following properties are true?\n\n- For any $A \\in P,$ we have $1 \\cdot A = A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) a_{ij} = \\alpha a_{ij} + \\beta a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "ff4fdeaf-bdb3-54d7-b5e9-2f3ad88b55a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9960" :question/problem "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $I_2 \\in M$\n\n- For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M.$\n\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ff2e01-5e8f-539f-a8de-2c9d6e6e074e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377328} {:answer/id #uuid "902ac293-50fe-5dd3-a103-1eb64f244826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377329} {:answer/id #uuid "36aad3d0-8ae8-5edd-ae55-22e11ce5fe77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377330} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a5a874-ca88-5ffe-9815-2312da44d691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377331} {:answer/id #uuid "b6450ae3-3fe9-5e6e-87f9-8a22693482e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2a5a874-ca88-5ffe-9815-2312da44d691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca9b6a6-d9cf-5797-968a-b4a9d7b52612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ae15ced-8119-5af5-8ffc-48ebd6ecda50" :question/math-academy-id "q-106688" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3cb7deb-769c-5697-9ab8-25c0643b3f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388250} {:answer/id #uuid "2b73b973-8729-56b1-bd66-c2f9040091a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388251} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cf7e2d-3418-5372-ad94-80fdd5ae01a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388252} {:answer/id #uuid "e785aa7c-f7b4-59f0-8fe7-6933c726aea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388253} {:answer/id #uuid "87f0b589-68fb-5896-8c78-bba15f07790e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b73b973-8729-56b1-bd66-c2f9040091a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388251} :answer-field/id #uuid "526fbdb6-202c-5b1d-a24d-d458ae47d76c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32414fd0-b715-59b8-9662-c3f08db22298" :question/math-academy-id "q-106700" :question/problem "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in M.$ Then $-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89561ae7-b58b-5494-97a4-ac6caf35ce31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388257} {:answer/id #uuid "bf98801f-8cd1-5f73-afef-61ffbe92ef36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388258} {:answer/id #uuid "07140bfc-36f9-52a2-a322-e3ac93bc42d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388259} {:answer/id #uuid "1cedddc5-0428-5f11-8c3f-285ce9624563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388260} {:answer/id #uuid "93388bb4-a19d-5497-9a09-f60a2172c78f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf98801f-8cd1-5f73-afef-61ffbe92ef36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388258} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d6cf17-ec29-5184-908b-67e882d88b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd9de429-1f02-596c-8b1c-229012ad598c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106710" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4c52d5a-0844-5105-ab53-9eb50de6ede1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121130} {:answer/id #uuid "eb97c9b4-7896-56b3-be8d-9d8f9d2670e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121131} {:answer/id #uuid "ff605ad5-60a7-5783-9696-ad7ac1f6d9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121132} {:answer/id #uuid "38dd97d7-614e-5024-b17b-a142e8e5b743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121133} {:answer/id #uuid "3fefeb7d-9f86-58ee-8a04-8ba57c157f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb97c9b4-7896-56b3-be8d-9d8f9d2670e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121131} :answer-field/id #uuid "97795195-e071-52ca-9410-8f360a4924cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a98e5d99-323b-544b-bce7-36fa3d3ba9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106806" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3b8bf9b-3359-58a2-81f8-44ba8f1a56b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322645} {:answer/id #uuid "acce2ad7-4ac7-5970-a743-89273d864945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322646} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4509b7-1334-5a79-9a08-5ad5b05b2e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322647} {:answer/id #uuid "7de40741-cf1e-5c83-89c4-65cbaa869262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322648} {:answer/id #uuid "8239421b-d8d3-5944-91c5-fe78a7c68f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acce2ad7-4ac7-5970-a743-89273d864945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322646} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa95a4ac-6bfb-5e7d-9774-948048284a6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f7c3c39-8a1a-5a06-82f0-07fdf953c652" :question/math-academy-id "q-160861" :question/problem "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbee640b-a95d-5f36-8f8c-3373fc92993b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322652} {:answer/id #uuid "048b116d-bb91-59ae-b961-45ef3fa4b588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee5c590-1042-5faa-b633-57fa5b5a8af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322654} {:answer/id #uuid "00dcddd8-ebfd-5c34-98ec-c9c1418284a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322655} {:answer/id #uuid "c8308f24-2099-5ec8-80b2-960d497009c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "048b116d-bb91-59ae-b961-45ef3fa4b588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653} :answer-field/id #uuid "12eab755-1d62-5455-bb52-18e7f27dab8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15b63522-0c12-5bc3-a318-dae5fc4b32c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160866" :question/problem "Let $W$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in W.$\n- The set $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)=(0)^{2}+1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple not in $W.$ For example, let $p(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $p(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $p(0)=1.$ However, \n\n$$\n2\\cdot p(0)=2\\cdot ({0}^{2}+1)=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n Hence, $k\\cdot p(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7ea0903-d431-594f-8c46-151fd8f520d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388264} {:answer/id #uuid "977f6cca-e778-56e0-aece-ed0ddb30f051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388265} {:answer/id #uuid "19125caf-a058-576b-9f3a-f954d21b7fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388266} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd17b85-3e53-53d1-8710-27afb2721fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388267} {:answer/id #uuid "d0826c64-a507-5ba4-99ab-04474c0955e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7ea0903-d431-594f-8c46-151fd8f520d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388264} :answer-field/id #uuid "5adb99a5-c1f3-537c-8592-8f3698140873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e779aef6-5e8f-5f83-b9cd-2d7a48b1b6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162379" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in N,$ where $p(x)={x}^{3}-1.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have, $p(x)={x}^{3}-1\\notin N$ because\n\n$$\np(0)=(0)^{3}-1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x),g(x)\\in N,$ where $f(0)=0$ and $g(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=0+0=0.\n$$\n\nHence $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0f06412-9f71-5a32-a3e8-ceaf2d01270b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388271} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3d99cf-68e5-5ac4-9700-da61ab8446a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388272} {:answer/id #uuid "a9845488-6106-50e3-9a49-2dcddf2b17e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388273} {:answer/id #uuid "64c9ca87-f925-59e3-ba64-032305a73b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388274} {:answer/id #uuid "3262dc00-40ac-5a79-8549-c4331b07b3b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c9ca87-f925-59e3-ba64-032305a73b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388274} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d10bce0-21bd-5047-91f5-ac58688f456e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0ad24e1-b135-5213-ba6a-3767c846e774" :question/math-academy-id "q-162620" :question/problem "Let $G$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero matrix $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $G.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in G,$ we have $A-O=A.$\n- For any $A,B\\in G,$ we have $A-B=B-A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,$O=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]$ with $a=b=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, as for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\nA-O=A+(-O)=A+O=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, let's consider the matrices $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then $O$ and ${I}_{2}$ belong to $G,$ and we\nhave \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}-O={I}_{2}\n$$\n\n \nbut \n\n$$\nO-{I}_{2}=-{I}_{2}\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8f6739-8478-50b4-a4df-9dd49545c279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388278} {:answer/id #uuid "c43a290b-75e4-5f7e-a211-9752f762f81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388279} {:answer/id #uuid "d224e21c-b849-5a4b-9377-ee7414d7e454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388280} {:answer/id #uuid "9e415153-8101-52d1-9917-58341ce04b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388281} {:answer/id #uuid "38ffd5f5-6944-5155-ad6d-0bf9b8a33d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c8f6739-8478-50b4-a4df-9dd49545c279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388278} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8182be3-8cab-53ef-95e4-5ed830799670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0988a68-ce20-56f3-895e-03e24d1dc996" :question/math-academy-id "q-162665" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\in S$\n- For any $p(x)\\in S,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot p(x)\\in S$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $deg(q)$ the degree of a polynomial $q.$ Then, for any $p(x)\\in S$ we have that $(-1)\\cdot p(x)$ is also a polynomial, and that\n\n$$\ndeg((-1)\\cdot p)=deg(-1)+deg(p)=0+deg(p)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)p(x)\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the values $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ac0766-2fb8-5197-94b9-fa2143aeadc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322687} {:answer/id #uuid "3b7e8965-d181-5f32-8451-0deac8c0cff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322688} {:answer/id #uuid "96f28374-50d2-5eb3-bddf-8e6eb514a5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322689} {:answer/id #uuid "91de564a-48cc-536d-8949-26f7ed45d9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322690} {:answer/id #uuid "01176b20-1014-5569-9e31-47827a7755e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b7e8965-d181-5f32-8451-0deac8c0cff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322688} :answer-field/id #uuid "9544c342-fa35-56f7-bcf9-490b56d4bcca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79a70322-eda4-52ca-9b62-4aa2732797d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-43859" :question/problem "Let $W$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$\n- For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={2}^{x}\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple *not* in $W.$ For example, let $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $f(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $f(0)=1.$ However,\n\n$$\n2\\cdot f(0)=2\\cdot {2}^{0}=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nHence, $k\\cdot f(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "655dccb1-b3b3-5aa5-8f2f-06abb4c197af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322694} {:answer/id #uuid "7df87f18-3990-52c0-bce9-cd0ffa554069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322695} {:answer/id #uuid "2da73cfc-c4fd-5400-bf14-ebf78d6216bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322696} {:answer/id #uuid "5c095616-2c46-5774-b4d5-60c2697ff444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322697} {:answer/id #uuid "ee59f476-3097-5a82-8125-a260dcbb7304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c095616-2c46-5774-b4d5-60c2697ff444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322697} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68bd26e-3789-535f-861e-cdf4cefee6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4de4ed8a-e25a-599c-88bb-6ed0a739caa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94649" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n- For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n- The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94b3a172-3584-5ffd-982f-0f3370f6e593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123965} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0af473-719f-51a5-a877-96dcd378fb16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123966} {:answer/id #uuid "eb14b59d-3f60-5be8-9b8f-51c1297ee0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123967} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4d25c4-8d53-58fe-8719-4a9708aef584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123968} {:answer/id #uuid "571c71e7-04c9-571d-b307-95bec7375fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94b3a172-3584-5ffd-982f-0f3370f6e593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123965} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e581fc2-2f3c-59f7-aa50-f3b3371d4f0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d4d76d9-0c38-5edc-a8b9-c85d1bae32b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94694" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $h(x)\\in U,$ where $h(x)=3x.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $U$ contains only polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $h(x)=3x$ is *not* a polynomial of this form. So, $h(x)\\notin U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x)=a{x}^{3}\\in U,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient, and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot p(x)=k(a{x}^{3})=(ka){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real coefficient, we conclude that $(ka){x}^{3}\\in U,$ i.e., $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82293cf7-076c-5d8a-9931-a4bc23990b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388285} {:answer/id #uuid "7340095c-282d-52a0-83b7-5b001b25310e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388286} {:answer/id #uuid "dde61d58-a800-5286-bc88-01ea0c6e5c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388287} {:answer/id #uuid "5f59ed22-fedf-5a93-9b18-52d268e80dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388288} {:answer/id #uuid "0142acc9-8d82-5511-aeeb-af41915406cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7340095c-282d-52a0-83b7-5b001b25310e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388286} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef3b1d1b-c70b-5761-a037-2adb230ae17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07c5aec0-d0c8-57f8-807e-d869fcad4620" :question/math-academy-id "q-94711" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in L,$ we have $2(p(x)+q(x))=2p(x)-2q(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=x+1$ and $q(x)=x-1$ from $L.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p(x)+q(x)) & =2(x+1+x-1) \\\\  & =2(2x) \\\\  & =4x \\\\ 2p(x)-2q(x) & =2(x+1)-2(x-1) \\\\  & =2x+2-2x+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e9adc8-3918-5315-a6ff-e82c12a245a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377356} {:answer/id #uuid "401b334b-5597-5b5f-82a4-25df094c9535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377357} {:answer/id #uuid "964c4be1-c9d8-5f55-9e39-b6f5c084dc77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377358} {:answer/id #uuid "24fe5ca6-4971-57b6-a136-75713e667059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377359} {:answer/id #uuid "30cf8744-11ec-5ba4-b6a7-c040b81cf95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e9adc8-3918-5315-a6ff-e82c12a245a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377356} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc5419d-eb1f-54ff-adcc-5bc70388ef65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe7eb437-c49e-5d72-9bf5-386650b87dda" :question/math-academy-id "q-94723" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2x\\in T.$\n- The scalar multiplication of polynomials in $T$ is associative.\n- For any $p(x)\\in T$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $2x$ is not a polynomial of this form. So, $2x\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8848460-74ea-55ec-b3a5-8170306c2dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123971} {:answer/id #uuid "e4be7317-37b4-5c33-b9fd-5c6ae3b75fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123972} {:answer/id #uuid "1685fa5f-62cf-55f2-ad2c-8231d3a0d4f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123973} {:answer/id #uuid "4e796911-c7db-5ac9-8449-1dcc4c3aeb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123974} {:answer/id #uuid "7ceda5ee-66ab-5a80-9fc2-e4e34c1edc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ceda5ee-66ab-5a80-9fc2-e4e34c1edc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123975} :answer-field/id #uuid "b32ef2aa-8a5c-5372-ab0e-6497823ac9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd415fe2-3ead-5cbb-88ba-59d9d1273317" :question/math-academy-id "q-94740" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0,\\infty )$ to $R$ of the form $f(x)=a{log}_{2}⁡(x),$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n- For any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$ and $f(x)\\in L,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $\\alpha \\in R$ and any $f(x),g(x)\\in L,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
